00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.200
Leah: Stacks, Queues and Lists are
more or less what they sound like.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.710
Jack: Firstly, a Stack is
the simplest grouping of data

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.470
and also the most important.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:14.209
Like in everyday life, a stack is a
group of objects stacked into a pile

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.570
and just as you wouldn’t pull the bottom
book out of a stack of books, the top

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:22.610
object in the stack or the last one
added, is always first to be removed.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.809
This principle is referred to
as LIFO or Last in First Out.

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:32.879
So, the book or object in this case
that was last added to the stack,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.759
would be the first to get removed.

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:39.610
A great example of a stack is the undo
button or back button on a browser,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.880
when clicked, you want to go back to
the last state or page, not the first.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.195
Leah: A Queue is similar to a
stack in that it is a grouping

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.760
of data but is different in the
method that data is retrieved.

00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:55.890
It works the same as in real life
for example when a queue is formed of

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.260
people waiting for something, the last
to join would not be the first to go.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.389
This is the same in coding, a
principle is used which is referred

00:01:03.389 --> 00:01:04.505
to as FIFO or First in First Out.

00:01:04.505 --> 00:01:04.726
The object in this case that is first
to enter the queue is the first to exit.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:17.030
A great example of a Queue is a
call waiting system at a call centre

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.070
where you want the first people who
dialled in, being the first callers

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.509
that are handled by your team.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.289
Jack: A Linear List is a more
memory intensive and a complicated

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:28.679
version of a stack or queue.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:32.030
While a stack or queue is only
ever as large as the objects

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.390
stacked or queued together, a list
is a prescribed amount of space.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.820
Just think about save slots for a game,
usually there is a set amount, and if

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.110
a player only ever saves their game
in the same slot overwriting their

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:49.869
previous save, all the memory allocated
to those other slots is sat wasted.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.500
Conversely if a player keeps saving
in a new slot each time, eventually

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.969
they will run out of slots so will
be unable to save any newer saves

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.470
without replacing older ones.

